<p>President Donald Trump said he wants to “cut off all trade with Spain” after Madrid limited US access to joint military bases that could support operations tied to strikes on Iran. The remarks also fit his broader complaints about Spain’s NATO defence spending. Trump made the comments at the White House during a meeting with German Chancellor Friedrich Merz, aiming his criticism at Spain’s government and arguing the US should rethink its economic relationship with the country. Spain pushed back publicly and, in reporting, appeared largely unfazed by the threat. It has framed both the base-access limits and its criticism of the Iran campaign as questions of sovereignty and international law. The episode shows how the Iran conflict is complicating transatlantic coordination, with Trump also criticizing the UK for not fully backing his military campaign.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/6IL1afM7cVEfTqjL43xZeW2YClQzysb0BBwK7GNuB3xRx13Net6W4jvBpzbigSLS4q1Nwfq7OfuUaaqgPS-ZA5kFpdSevF6I_AQ1zoeW5kZ-tYcV8j_dR2gIs3Z9FW7eTR0sCaJS2woYCYh6bWy7ylxbxSn852z6CQlAc-di07PXVRLIqdy7PExzWX7Tz4ELMyDWh1VFEC1li3hfkVYQBw' alt='President Trump had several complaints about Spain, including that it has not increased its NATO defense spending to a level he set.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Bases and aircraft: As the dispute over access unfolded, the US relocated 15 aircraft — including refuelling tankers — from bases in southern Spain.</li><li>UK posture: Trump also criticized the UK’s level and timing of support for the Iran campaign. Prime Minister Keir Starmer said he did not believe in “regime change from the skies,” and later announced additional protective deployments to Cyprus, including HMS Dragon and helicopters with counter-drone capabilities.</li><li>Treasury direction: El País reported Trump said he instructed Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent to cut all trade ties with Spain.</li><li>Spain’s exposure: The Straits Times reported Spain may be less vulnerable to US economic punishment than some other European countries, even as Trump threatened a total trade cutoff.</li></ul><blockquote>We’re going to cut off all trade with Spain. We don’t want anything to do with Spain. - Donald Trump</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Spanish government: Spain’s leadership condemned the US-Israeli attack on Iran as a breach of international law and, according to multiple reports, restricted US access to bases for missions linked to the Iran strikes. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1978099/trump-not-happy-with-spain-uk-over-iran'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>Spanish Foreign Ministry: Foreign Minister José Manuel Albares said Spain would not allow the US to use Naval Station Rota or Morón Air Base for missions linked to the Iran conflict, adding the bases were not used for the opening strikes. (<a href='https://www.rt.com/news/633671-trump-cut-trade-spain/'>rt.com</a>)</li><li>German government: Meeting Trump at the White House, Chancellor Friedrich Merz said the Iran war was hurting European economies. (<a href='https://www.semafor.com/article/03/03/2026/trump-slams-spain-uk-over-iran-stance'>semafor.com</a>)</li><li>New York Times reporting: Spanish officials “didn’t seem fazed” by Trump’s trade threat, the paper reported, suggesting Madrid expects limited follow-through or impact. (<a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/03/03/world/middleeast/trump-spain-trade.html'>nytimes.com</a>)</li><li>BBC analysis: The BBC’s Europe editor said Europe has struggled to speak with one voice on the Middle East conflict, looking uncoordinated and short on leverage. (<a href='https://www.semafor.com/article/03/03/2026/trump-slams-spain-uk-over-iran-stance'>semafor.com</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='http://www.euronews.com/2026/03/03/trump-lashes-out-at-spain-says-he-is-going-to-cut-off-all-trade'>Trump lashes out at Spain, says he is &#x27;going to cut off all trade&#x27;</a> - euronews.com</li><li><a href='https://www.semafor.com/article/03/03/2